===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} A [[building#Noun|building]] [[material#Noun|material]] created by mixing [[cement#Noun|cement]], [[water#Noun|water]], and [[aggregate#Noun|aggregate]] such as [[gravel#Noun|gravel]] and [[sand#Noun|sand]].
#: {{ux|en|The road was made of '''concrete''' that had been poured in large slabs.}}
#* {{RQ:Wired|author=Brandon Keim|quotee=Hendrik Van Oss|title=Space-Age Concrete the Answer for Failing Bridges?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210619030945/https://www.wired.com/2007/08/space-age-concr/|date=2 August 2007|passage=In the next few decades, says Van Oss, building codes will change, opening the way for innovative materials. But while new '''concretes''' may be stronger and more durable, they are also more expensive - and whether the tendency of developers and the public to focus on short-term rather than long-term costs will also change is another matter.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ben Mussett|title='As ugly as ugly can be': Why are there still temporary barriers around Union Station four years later?|work={{w|Toronto Star}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230826205146/https://www.thestar.com/news/gta/as-ugly-as-ugly-can-be-why-are-there-still-temporary-barriers-around-union-station/article_2341a141-cc58-573b-ad7e-f9418675c504.html|location=Toronto, ON|publisher={{w|Toronto Star Newspapers Ltd.}}|date=7 October 2022|issn=0319-0781|oclc=1081127642|passage=Within hours of the deadly van attack on April 23, 2018, the city installed a series of thigh-high '''concrete''' barriers around Union Station and other bustling spots in downtown Toronto.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} A [[term]] designating both a [[quality]] and the [[subject]] in which it exists; a concrete term.
# [[sugar#Noun|Sugar]] [[boil#Verb|boiled]] down from [[cane#Noun|cane]] [[juice#Noun|juice]] to a solid mass.
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[dessert#Noun|dessert]] of [[frozen custard#Noun|frozen custard]] with various [[topping#Noun|toppings]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Lutz|authorlink=John Lutz (mystery writer)|title=Diamond Eyes|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Thomas Dunne Books}}|year=1990|page=170|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/diamondeyes00lutz/page/170/mode/1up|isbn=0-312-05074-7|passage=When Nudger and Claudia were finished eating they drove to the Ted Drewes frozen custard stand on Chippewa and stood in line for a couple of chocolate chip '''concretes'''. Drewes's '''concretes''' were delicious custard concoctions so thick that before the kids working behind the counter handed them to customers, they turned the cups upside down to demonstrate that the contents wouldn't pour out.}}
#* {{RQ:WaPo|author=Candy Sagon|title=Nielsen's Frozen Custard in Vienna|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/food/2003/07/16/nielsens-frozen-custard-in-vienna/7bf574d9-e175-4af1-9e08-dc518ae0e759/|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/PQHjr|archivedate=26 August 2023|date=16 July 2003|passage=Once there, she opts for a '''concrete''' -- Nielsen's thick, spoonable, frozen vanilla custard mixed with add-ins like Oreos or strawberries or chocolate chips and piled into a 16-ounce cup for $4.35. She can't eat it all, of course, which is why a couple of friends need to come along as well.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Allie Chanthorn Reinmann|title=The Difference Between Milkshakes and Concretes (and How to Make Them)|work={{w|Lifehacker}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608020935/https://lifehacker.com/the-difference-between-milkshakes-and-concretes-and-ho-1850424489|date=11 May 2023|passage=A '''concrete''' has some distinct differences from a milkshake, specifically, the custard base mixture, the final texture, and the mix-ins. Technically, the common ice cream you buy at the store and use in a regular milkshake is made from a custard base. A custard is dairy thickened with the help of heated whole eggs or egg yolks, and a '''concrete''' uses a custard base that has a higher ratio of egg yolks in the recipe than the average ice cream.}}
# {{senseid|en|semi-solid extract}} {{lb|en|chemistry}} An extract of herbal materials that has a semi-solid consistency, especially when such materials are partly aromatic.
# {{senseid|en|obsolete concretion}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} Any [[solid#Adjective|solid]] [[mass#Noun|mass]] formed by the [[coalescence#Noun|coalescence]] of separate [[particles#Noun|particle]]; a compound substance, a [[concretion#Noun|concretion]].
#* {{RQ:Boyle Sceptical Chymist|page=26|passage=And firſt, if I would now deal rigidly with my Adverſary, I might here make a great Queſtion of the very way of Probation which he and others employ, without the leaſt ſcruple, to evince, that the Bodies commonly cali'd mixt, are made up of Earth, Air, Water, and Fire, which they are pleas'd alſo to call Elements; namely that upon the ſuppos'd ''Analyſis'' made by the fire, of the former ſort of '''''Concretes''''', there are wont to emerge Bodies reſembling thoſe which they take for the Elements.}}
#* {{RQ:Hooke Micrographia|page=13|passage=Of this kind, I ſuppoſe, the ''Æther'', that is the ''medium'' or ''fluid'' body, in which all other bodies do as it were ſwim and move; and particularly, the ''Air'', which ſeems nothing elſe but a kind of ''tincture'' or ''ſolution'' of terreſtrial and aqueous particles ''diſſolv'd'' into it, and agitated by it, juſt as the ''tincture'' of ''Cocheneel'' is nothing but ſome finer ''diſſoluble'' parts of that '''Concrete''' lick'd up or ''diſſolv'd'' by the ''fluid'' water.}}

